{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-07-03T08:57:55.988Z","sessionId":"7aba7344-6e98-4c00-b7e9-ad132cfe087b","content":"while this is running I did some research and what do you think about creating a new library that can replace our neo4j the idea is compression and speed, Architectural Design Authority: S16 Binary Knowledge Store (Project Prime-Silo)\nExecutive Summary and Technical Feasibility Evaluation\nTransitioning the data persistence layer of Project Prime-Silo from a traditional graph-based topology to an embedded, bare-metal, binary-quantized storage engine is highly feasible and structurally necessary to sustain the continuous multi-modal experiences required by deterministic multi-agent swarms. Graph databases like Neo4j introduce severe write latency, substantial memory footprints, and heavy CPU serialization overhead when handling continuous high-throughput streams of unstructured multi-modal inputs, dense vector coordinates, and system state buffers. By separating key-value indices from raw payloads and executing binary-quantized vector matches directly inside the model's runtime execution session, the S16 Binary Knowledge Store establishes a zero-latency memory mesh optimized for resource-constrained edge computing architectures. However, a critical technical contradiction exists in the Product Requirements Document (PRD) regarding the execution architecture of the dynamic routing phase. The PRD specifies that when a local Neural Processing Unit is detected, heavy computations—including vector graph traversal (Hamming distance) and LZ4 payload decompression—should be routed directly to the silicon accelerator. In real-world systems, NPUs are highly specialized, statically compiled matrix engines designed exclusively for dense tensor operations like general matrix multiplications and convolutions. They lack the programmable instruction pipelines and arbitrary pointer arithmetic required to run file-system operations, sequential compression runtimes, or non-deterministic graph traversals. Attempting to compile the Hierarchical Navigable Small World index traversal or the LZ4 decompression logic to run directly on NPU Execution Providers via OpenVINO, Qualcomm Neural Processing SDK (QNN), or CoreML will trigger immediate compilation failures, forcing execution back to the CPU. The Architectural Design Authority recommends a revised dynamic routing strategy. The dynamic hardware topology check must route the static mathematical tensor operations—specifically, the extraction of high-dimensional embeddings and their subsequent 1-bit binary quantization—to the local NPU. Conversely, the stateful database point-lookups, HNSW graph traversal via SimSIMD, pointer resolution, and sequential LZ4 decompression must be routed to the host CPU, utilizing specialized Single Instruction Multiple Data registers, or offloaded to General-Purpose Graphics Processing Unit architectures. The table below summarizes the feasibility, implementation strategies, and trade-offs of the core system requirements: | Requirement | Feasibility | Technical Execution Strategy | Primary Risks and Architectural Mitigations | |---|---|---|---| | FR1: Wire-Speed Ingestion & KV Separation | Highly Feasible | Decouple the write path using RocksDB BlobDB to write keys to the LSM-tree and append raw multi-modal payloads to sequential blob files. | Compaction-induced write stalls under high ingestion loads. Prevent by disabling WAL during bulk loads and using a vector-based MemTable. | | FR2: Lazy Zero-Copy Decompression | Highly Feasible | Use RocksDB's native PinnableSlice to pin block-cache buffers in memory and execute LZ4_decompress_safe directly into pre-allocated ONNX Runtime output tensors. | Heap fragmentation and buffer copy overhead. Prevent by avoiding standard library heap allocations and relying strictly on the ONNX memory allocator. | | FR3: Hardware-Agnostic Routing | Partially Feasible | Compile static embedding and quantization steps to the NPU. Dynamic vector search and decompression must fallback to CPU SIMD or GPU. | Compilation failures on NPUs due to dynamic sequence shapes. Mitigation: Configure fixed-bound input dimensions and explicit CPU-fallback regions. | | NFR1: Determinism & Sovereignty | Highly Feasible | Statically link RocksDB, USearch, and the ONNX Runtime into a single, zero-dependency C++ distributable binary. | Index topology variance on multi-threaded builds. Mitigation: Restrict HNSW graph construction to a single-threaded execution queue. |\nStorage Layer Architecture: Key-Value Separation and Engine Analysis\nDesigning a bare-metal persistence layer for multi-agent experience logs requires balancing high ingestion rates, immediate point-lookups, and disk storage efficiency. Traditional Log-Structured Merge-tree storage engines buffer incoming writes in memory before flushing them to disk as sorted files. Background compactions continually merge these files to maintain ordered levels, but this process continuously copies large values across disk sectors, leading to severe write amplification, hardware degradation, and system write stalls.\nEngine Performance Evaluation\nAn evaluation of storage engines confirms that memory-mapped B+ Tree databases, such as the Lightning Memory-Mapped Database (LMDB), deliver optimal random read latency but suffer from severe write amplification, poor sequential load times, and a lack of built-in compression. Additionally, Go-based options like BadgerDB avoid Cgo-overhead but introduce runtime garbage collection spikes that disrupt deterministic execution guarantees. The table below compares these engines under representative workloads: | Performance Metric | LMDB (v0.9.24) | RocksDB (v10.10.1) | BadgerDB (Pure Go) | |---|---|---|---| | Sequential Write Throughput | 2.38 \\text{ Mops/sec} | 2.11 \\text{ Mops/sec} | Moderate (Sequential LSM) | | Random Write Throughput | 0.77 \\text{ Mops/sec} | [span_84](https://notebooklm.google.com/start_span)[span_84](https://notebooklm.google.com/end_span)1.98 \\text{ Mops/sec} | High (Optimized batching) | | Random Read Throughput | 5.91 \\text{ Mops/sec} | 0.99[span_86](https://notebooklm.google.com/start_span)[span_86](https://notebooklm.google.com/end_span) \\text{ Mops/sec} | Low-to-Moderate | | Average Read Latency | 1.10\\ \\mu\\text{s} | 7.52\\ \\m[span_88](https://notebooklm.google.com/start_span)[span_88](https://notebooklm.google.com/end_span)u\\text{s} | Moderate-to-High | | Write Amplification Ratio | 1.64\\text{--}1.80\\times | 1.23\\text{--[span_90](https://notebooklm.google.com/start_span)[span_90](https://notebooklm.google.com/end_span)}1.41\\times | Highly Reduced (Key-Separated) | | Memory Footprint (RSS) | 3194\\text{--}3373 \\text{ MB} | 246\\text{--}308 [span_92](https://notebooklm.google.com/start_span)[span_92](https://notebooklm.google.com/end_span)\\text{ MB} | High (Go runtime buffer) | | Storage Reclamation Efficiency | None (Files only expand on delete) | High (Compaction filters) | High (Background value GC) |\nWiscKey Mechanics and Integrated BlobDB\nTo resolve the write amplification limitations of traditional LSM storage, the S16 Binary Knowledge Store implements key-value separation based on WiscKey principles, executed natively through RocksDB's integrated BlobDB. When the multi-agent write path ingests an experience log, the raw payload (such as images, system states, or dense floating-point context arrays) is compressed using LZ4 and written sequentially to append-only blob files on disk. Concurrently, the primary LSM-tree registers only the key (representing a unique 64-bit vector ID) and a value pointer. This pointer is structured as a small key-address index entry containing the active blob file number and the exact byte offset: Because the size of the key-address entries in the SST files is highly compressed, the LSM-tree remains exceptionally small. Background compactions are restricted to sorting tiny pointers, reducing total write amplification by up to 90%, preventing write stalls under continuous ingestion, and extending SSD lifespan.\nSpace Amplification and Garbage Collection Mappings\nWhile separating keys and values optimizes the ingestion path, it introduces space amplification. Because RocksDB is append-only, updates or deletions of keys leave unreferenced (garbage) data inside the immutable blob files. Without active garbage collection, deleted agent experiences are never reclaimed, causing the database to grow indefinitely. Compaction-triggered GC policies help address this but can cause up to 3.4\\times space amplification under small-value workloads because value files must wait for LSM compactions to run. Additionally, deploying key-value separation over Zoned Namespaces (ZNS) using user-level file systems like ZenFS introduces a \"garbage-collection-over-garbage-collection\" (GoG) issue, where application-level value GC and flash-level zone cleaning operate independently, causing redundant memory copying. To mitigate space amplification, the system must enable RocksDB's native background Garbage Collector, configuring the oldest 25% of blob files as GC candidates. This ensures that valid blobs are relocated and rewritten to new blob files during standard compactions, while unreferenced data is safely discarded. To align with ZenFS requirements, the BlobDB-Aware Zone Allocation (BAZA) algorithm should be implemented to map blob files sequentially to flash zones based on their creation order. The table below outlines the recommended C++ parameters for the S16 RocksDB storage layer to balance write and space amplification: | C++ Configuration Option | Recommended Value | Engineering Rationale | |---|---|---| | cf_opts.enable_blob_files | true | Enables key-value separation inside the Column Family. | | cf_opts.min_blob_size | 1024 (1 KB) | Keeps data smaller than 1 KB in the SST files to prevent excessive disk-seek overhead for small metadata payloads. | | | cf_opts.enable_blob_garbage_collection | true | | cf_opts.blob_garbag[span_60](https://notebooklm.google.com/start_span)[span_60](https://notebooklm.google.com/end_span)[span_69](https://notebooklm.google.com/start_span)[span_69](https://notebooklm.google.com/end_span)e_collection_age_cutoff | 0.25 | Targets the oldest 25% of files for GC, capping CPU and I/O overhead during compaction. | | cf_opts.blob_file_size | 134217728 (128 MB) | Large sequential writes match optimal disk blocks. | | cf_opts.write_buffer_size | 134217728 (128 MB) | MemTable sizing; ensures dense, sorted keys are written in parallel. | | cf_opts.target_file_size_base | 8388608 (8 MB) | Configures a clean, leveled LSM layout for rapid pointer sorting. |\nHigh-Performance Vector Index Layer and Binary Quantization\nTo support infinite local scaling on edge devices, the S16 vector index layer cannot store full-precision 1536-dimensional floating-point arrays, which require 6048 bytes of memory per vector. Instead, it implements 1-bit binary quantization to compress vectors into compact bitwise representations.\nMathematical Mechanics of 1-Bit Quantization\nLet \\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^D represent a high-dimensional, full-precision dense embedding vector. To project this vector into a 1-bit quantized space, we apply sign-based thresholding, mapping each dimension to a single bit b_i \\in {0, 1}: For a 1536-dimension embedding vector, this transformation compresses the vector into a 1536-bit representation, packing the bits into 192 unsigned 8-bit bytes (using standard bit-packing operations like np.packbits): The semantic similarity between two binary-quantized vectors \\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in {0, 1}^D is calculated using the normalized Hamming distance d_H(\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}), which represents the proportion of differing bits: According to the Chernoff bound, the normalized Hamming distance is an unbiased estimator of the angular distance (the angle \\theta in radians) between the original high-dimensional vectors, preserving semantic search quality: This mathematical property ensures that relative vector distances are preserved. At scale, this representation achieves 90\\text{--}95% search accuracy while significantly reducing processing overhead and RAM utilization.\nHardware-Accelerated Bitwise Computations\nAt runtime, calculating the Hamming distance between the 1-bit quantized search query and the indexed vectors bypasses floating-point arithmetic pipelines. The distance calculation is mapped directly to hardware-accelerated bitwise instructions :\n\n1. Bitwise XOR (^): Identifies positions where the bits differ.\n2. Population Count (popcount): Sums the number of set bits. On modern x86 architectures (such as Intel 4th Generation Xeon and Core Ultra Series), the AVX-512 instruction set features the native instruction __mm512_popcnt_epi64. This instruction computes the population count of eight 64-bit integers simultaneously in a single clock cycle, reducing the execution path length by 8\\times. On ARM AArch64 architectures (such as Apple Silicon or AWS Graviton), hardware acceleration utilizes NEON or the Scalable Vector Extension (SVE). NEON uses 128-bit vector registers with the vcntq_u8 instruction. SVE implements vector-length agnostic (VLA) execution, allowing code to scale dynamically across hardware register widths from 128 to 2048 bits without recompilation.\nUSearch, SimSIMD, and FAISS Integrations\nWhile FAISS remains a common industry standard for high-performance vector searches, it features a heavy codebase with multiple dependencies. In contrast, USearch is an optimized, single-header C++11 HNSW engine designed for lightweight, zero-dependency deployments. | Architectural Feature | FAISS Indexing Engine | USearch Vector Engine | |---|---|---| | Codebase Sizing | \\approx 84,000 \\text{ Source Lines} | \\approx 3,000 \\text{ Source Lines} | | Quantization Methods | Downcasting, Learned Product Quantization | Downcasting, Hand-aligned bit packing | | Custom User Metrics | Highly Restricted | Unrestricted (SimSIMD callback) | | External Dependencies | BLAS, OpenMP | None (Header-only compilation) | | Disk Serialization | Partial (File write) | Full Memory-mapped zero-RAM restore (view=True) | | ID Key Representation | Stiff 63-bit signed integer keys | Efficient 32-bit, 40-bit, or 64-bit labels | To maximize vector-matching efficiency on the host CPU, USearch integrates SimSIMD, bypassing compiler auto-vectorization limits. Compilers often struggle with loop auto-vectorization due to data dependencies, pointer aliasing, and tail-loop branching penalties. SimSIMD addresses this by providing hand-written assembly kernels that use masked vector loads to process remaining vector elements without branching penalties, achieving near-memcpy speed.\nDeterminism and Graph Topology\nA critical requirement for localized multi-agent swarms is system determinism. Standard parallel graph construction algorithms used in engines like USearch and hnswlib create a non-deterministic insertion order due to race conditions in thread scheduling. Because the HNSW graph's topology depends directly on the insertion order, building the index across multiple threads can generate different graph structures and search results on different runs. To guarantee absolute repeatability across instances, S16 uses single-threaded index construction, trading parallel build performance for deterministic, bit-for-bit reproducible index topologies on every machine, matching the design principles of engines like MonaVec.\nStateful ONNX Runtime Custom Operator Architecture\nTo eliminate CPU serialization, process context switching, and the overhead of calling external database services, the S16 architecture encapsulates RocksDB and the USearch index within a stateful, custom C++ operator registered directly inside the local ONNX Runtime execution session. The custom operator, named MeshMemoryRetrieve, acts as the stateful bridge between the stateless layers of the ONNX computational graph and the persistent key-value store on disk.\nStateful Struct Lifecycles\nCustom operators in the ONNX Runtime can be implemented as stateful C++ structs that persist across inference calls. This stateful design allows the operator to maintain persistent database connections and memory-mapped index pointers across multiple inference requests. During the session initialization phase, the struct constructor runs, reading configuration parameters (such as top_k) directly from the ONNX model graph attributes using the OrtApi and OrtKernelInfo interfaces. The operator holds these values in member variables and preserves the pointers to the shared RocksDB database and USearch graph, ensuring they remain alive for the duration of the parent session.\n\n```\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                    ONNX Runtime Session                      |\n|                                                              |\n|  +--------------------+             +---------------------+  |\n|  | Input Query Tensor |             | Output Context      |  |\n|  | (1-bit Quantized)  |             | Tensor Buffer       |  |\n|  +--------------------+             +---------------------+  |\n|            |                                   ^             |\n|            v                                   |             |\n|  +--------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|  |           MeshMemoryRetrieve (Stateful Custom Op)      |  |\n|  |                                                        |  |\n|  |  1. Read 1-bit query tensor                            |  |\n|  |  2. HNSW Hamming search on USearch index               |  |\n|  |  3. Get target vector ID (label)                       |  |\n|  |  4. Point lookup on RocksDB using PinnableSlice       |  |\n|  |  5. Lazy LZ4 decompress directly into Output Buffer    |  |\n|  +--------------------------------------------------------+  |\n|         |                                      ^             |\n|         v                                      |             |\n|  +--------------+                      +---------------+     |\n|  | USearch Index|                      | RocksDB Cache |     |\n|  | (Mem-mapped) |                      | (Pinned block)|     |\n|  +--------------+                      +---------------+     |\n+--------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n\n```\n\nZero-Copy Memory Pipelines and Decompression\nTo minimize latency during the retrieval phase, the C++ custom operator implements a zero-copy pipeline that accesses raw disk data and decompresses it directly into the active model's execution memory :\n\n1. RocksDB Cache Pinning: When querying RocksDB using the retrieved vector ID, the operator uses a PinnableSlice. If the target block is already present in RocksDB's block cache, the database pins the block in memory and returns a direct reference, avoiding an intermediate memory copy.\n2. Buffer Extraction: The operator reads the compressed block's header to resolve the original uncompressed payload size. It then bypasses standard C++ library heap allocations and allocates the target output tensor directly in the ONNX Runtime execution memory using Ort::Custom::Tensor::Allocate.\n3. Decompression: The operator runs sequential LZ4 safe block decompression (LZ4_decompress_safe) using the pinned database cache buffer as the source and the pre-allocated ONNX tensor buffer as the destination :\n\n```\nint decompressed_bytes = LZ4_decompress_safe(\n    compressed_source_ptr,\n    target_onnx_tensor_ptr,\n    compressed_length,\n    original_length\n);\n``` [span_225](start_span)[span_225](end_span)[span_226](start_span)[span_226](end_span)\n\nThis design allows the retrieved context to be injected directly into the local model's attention window with zero intermediate memory copies or serialization overhead.[span_227](start_span)[span_227](end_span)[span_228](start_span)[span_228](end_span)\n\n### Memory Management and Arena Allocations\nManaging memory allocation patterns is critical to preventing heap fragmentation on edge devices.[span_229](start_span)[span_229](end_span) By default, the ONNX Runtime memory arena does not release memory back to the operating system after an inference run.[span_230](start_span)[span_230](end_span) \n\nTo prevent memory bloat during large retrieval requests, the S16 architecture uses a shared allocator across ONNX sessions, configured with the `kSameAsRequested` arena extension strategy.[span_231](start_span)[span_231](end_span) \n\nThis ensures that memory is allocated in exact, predictable blocks for each request rather than doubling in size over time, keeping the memory footprint stable.[span_232](start_span)[span_232](end_span)\n\nThe C++ code below implements this stateful custom operator:\n\n```cpp\n#include \"onnxruntime_cxx_api.h\"\n#include \"rocksdb/db.h\"\n#include \"usearch/index.hpp\"\n#include \"lz4.h\"\n#include <string_view>\n#include <memory>\n#include <stdexcept>\n\n// Stateful custom operator implementation for the ONNX Runtime\nstruct MeshMemoryRetrieveKernel {\n    // Constructor receives global pointers to pre-loaded resources\n    MeshMemoryRetrieveKernel(const OrtApi* ort_api, const OrtKernelInfo* info, \n                             rocksdb::DB* shared_db, \n                             unum::usearch::index_gt<unum::usearch::hamming_gt<uint8_t>>* shared_index)\n        : db_(shared_db), index_(shared_index) {\n        \n        // Extract static operational attributes defined in the ONNX graph\n        int64_t top_k_attr;\n        OrtStatus* status = ort_api->KernelInfoGetAttribute_int64(info, \"top_k\", &top_k_attr);\n        if (status!= nullptr) {\n            top_k_ = 1; // Default fallback fallback\n            ort_api->ReleaseStatus(status);\n        } else {\n            top_k_ = static_cast<size_t>(top_k_attr);\n        }\n    }\n\n    // Core execution node triggered during session evaluation\n    void Compute(OrtKernelContext* context) {\n        Ort::KernelContext ctx(context);\n        \n        // Step 1: Access 1-bit input query vector (zero-copy read from the graph)\n        auto query_tensor = ctx.GetInput(0);\n        const uint8_t* quantized_query = query_tensor.GetTensorData<uint8_t>();\n        size_t query_dims = query_tensor.GetTensorTypeAndShapeInfo().GetElementCount();\n\n        // Step 2: Query USearch index utilizing SimSIMD Hamming metric\n        auto search_results = index_->search(quantized_query, top_k_);\n        if (search_results.size() == 0) {\n            throw std::runtime_error(\"No matching semantic vectors found in index.\");\n        }\n\n        // Retrieve the optimal key (vector ID mapped to RocksDB)\n        uint64_t matched_id = search_results.member.label;\n\n        // Step 3: Lazy point-lookup into RocksDB with PinnableSlice to avoid copies\n        rocksdb::PinnableSlice compressed_blob;\n        rocksdb::ReadOptions read_options;\n        read_options.verify_checksums = true; // Guard data integrity on disk\n\n        rocksdb::Slice db_key(reinterpret_cast<const char*>(&matched_id), sizeof(matched_id));\n        rocksdb::Status s = db_->Get(read_options, db_->DefaultColumnFamily(), db_key, &compressed_blob);\n        \n        if (!s.ok()) {\n            throw std::runtime_error(\"RocksDB pointer resolution failed: \" + s.ToString());\n        }\n\n        // Parse compressed blob header to extract original uncompressed size\n        int32_t original_payload_size = *reinterpret_cast<const int32_t*>(compressed_blob.data());\n        const char* lz4_payload_start = compressed_blob.data() + sizeof(int32_t);\n        size_t compressed_bytes_len = compressed_blob.size() - sizeof(int32_t);\n\n        // Step 4: Allocate the target output context tensor directly in ONNX runtime memory\n        std::vector<int64_t> output_shape = { 1, static_cast<int64_t>(original_payload_size) };\n        auto output_tensor = ctx.GetOutput(0, output_shape.data(), output_shape.size());\n        char* active_context_window = output_tensor.GetTensorMutableData<char>();\n\n        // Step 5: Zero-copy decompression directly into the active inference context window\n        int decompressed_bytes = LZ4_decompress_safe(\n            lz4_payload_start, \n            active_context_window, \n            static_cast<int>(compressed_bytes_len), \n            static_cast<int>(original_payload_size)\n        );\n\n        if (decompressed_bytes < 0) {\n            throw std::runtime_error(\"Zero-copy LZ4 decompression failed or corrupted payload.\");\n        }\n    }\n\nprivate:\n    rocksdb::DB* db_;\n    unum::usearch::index_gt<unum::usearch::hamming_gt<uint8_t>>* index_;\n    size_t top_k_ = 1;\n};\n``` [span_234](start_span)[span_234](end_span)[span_235](start_span)[span_235](end_span)[span_236](start_span)[span_236](end_span)[span_237](start_span)[span_237](end_span)\n\n---\n\n## Dynamic Hardware Topology Routing and Fallback Mechanics\n\nTo achieve high energy efficiency, the S16 Binary Knowledge Store must optimize execution across heterogeneous edge hardware platforms.[span_238](start_span)[span_238](end_span)[span_239](start_span)[span_239](end_span) At system startup, the base orchestrator executes a dynamic hardware topology check, querying the system to detect NPUs (via Intel OpenVINO, Qualcomm QNN, or Apple CoreML), integrated or discrete GPUs, and SIMD-capable host CPUs.[span_240](start_span)[span_240](end_span)[span_241](start_span)[span_241](end_span)[span_242](start_span)[span_242](end_span)[span_243](start_span)[span_243](end_span)[span_244](start_span)[span_244](end_span)\n\n### Dynamic Routing Strategy\nWhile NPUs provide excellent energy efficiency and throughput for static, dense neural network operations, they lack general-purpose programmability. \n\nAdditionally, NPU compilers require static tensor shapes, which prevents them from executing the dynamic sequence lengths and autoregressive key-value (KV) cache updates required by language models.[span_245](start_span)[span_245](end_span)[span_246](start_span)[span_246](end_span) Attempting to run dynamic autoregressive steps on an NPU triggers compiler failures or defaults to slow CPU execution.[span_247](start_span)[span_247](end_span)\n\n\n\n```\n\n+---------------------------------------+ | Base System Orchestrator | | (Dynamic Hardware Query) | +---------------------------------------+ | v +---------------------------------------+ | Dynamic Topology Check | +---------------------------------------+ | +-------------------------+-------------------------+ | | v v +------------------------------------+ +------------------------------------+ | Embedding & Quantization Nodes | | Embedding & Quantization Nodes | | - Compiled AOT & run on NPU | | - Executed on Host CPU | | (OpenVINO / QNN / CoreML) | | (Optimized SIMD: AVX-512/NEON) | +------------------------------------+ +------------------------------------+ | | +-------------------------+-------------------------+ | v +---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ | Stateful Index & Retrieve Operations | | - RocksDB key-pointer lookup, USearch HNSW graph traversal, & LZ4 decompression | | - Stateful C++ Custom Operator running directly on CPU SIMD or GPU | +---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ | v +---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+ | Generative Context Decoding (LLM) | | - Dynamic autoregressive sequence processing | | - Executed on host CPU (XNNPACK / KleidiAI) or local GPU (Metal / Vulkan) | +---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n```\n\nTo resolve these platform constraints, the system uses a hybrid execution model:\n1. **Static Pre-Processing (NPU)**: High-dimensional embedding generation and sign-based 1-bit quantization are compiled into static-shape graphs and offloaded to the local NPU.\n2. **Stateful Graph Search & Decompression (CPU/GPU)**: The `MeshMemoryRetrieve` custom operator, which handles background key point-lookups and LZ4 block decompression, is routed to the host CPU.[span_248](start_span)[span_248](end_span)[span_249](start_span)[span_249](end_span) This process uses SimSIMD to leverage hardware registers (such as AVX-512 popcount or ARM NEON/SVE).[span_250](start_span)[span_250](end_span)[span_251](start_span)[span_251](end_span)[span_252](start_span)[span_252](end_span)\n3. **Autoregressive Decoding (CPU/GPU Fallback)**: The downstream generative LLM layers, which process dynamic context lengths, execute on the host CPU (via XNNPACK/KleidiAI) or the integrated GPU (via Metal/Vulkan compute shaders).[span_253](start_span)[span_253](end_span)[span_256](start_span)[span_256](end_span)\n\n### Unified Fallback Chains\nTo ensure stable execution on devices without NPUs, the system defines fallback chains for each hardware provider:\n\n* **CoreML Fallback (Apple Silicon)**: The runtime initializes the CoreML delegate targeting the Apple Neural Engine (AN[span_18](start_span)[span_18](end_span)E).[span_259](start_span)[span_259](end_span)[span_261](start_span)[span_261](end_span) If unsupported dynamic operations or custom C++ operators are encountered, CoreML falls back to the Metal GPU delegate before falling back to the CPU.[span_260](start_span)[span_260](end_span)[span_262](start_span)[span_262](end_span)\n* **OpenVINO Fallback (Intel Core Ultra)**: The runtime compiles the static subgraphs using OpenVINO's heterogeneous execution modes.[span_263](start_span)[span_263](end_span)[span_264](start_span)[span_264](end_span) Using OpenVINO's `AUTO` plugin configuration, the system registers devices in priority order:\n\n$$\\text{Device Sequence} = \\text{AUTO:NPU,GPU,CPU}$$ [span_265](start_span)[span_265](end_span)\n\nTo prev