he_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1618},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Ccet7tWJzFaH9NHMrRGXJ","attributionMcpServer":"ccd_session","attributionMcpTool":"mark_chapter","type":"assistant","uuid":"8616a21d-81cf-4d82-a68d-e6d2373b1387","timestamp":"2026-07-03T09:24:44.214Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\Users\\nsdha\\OneDrive\\binary16\\s16","sessionId":"7aba7344-6e98-4c00-b7e9-ad132cfe087b","version":"2.1.197","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
{"parentUuid":"8616a21d-81cf-4d82-a68d-e6d2373b1387","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6b3b69d6-e951-4248-8963-c1572fbd5abb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TqP921YfaHFPfPsY85huTk","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\nsdha\\OneDrive\\binary16\\s16\\docs\\ADR-002-vector-index.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"2c44f6e0-c2e3-41f1-9227-5f28d6fc8d33","timestamp":"2026-07-03T09:24:44.287Z","toolUseResult":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\nsdha\\OneDrive\\binary16\\s16\\docs\\ADR-002-vector-index.md","oldString":"4. **Rescore (FR4):** search fetches `k × oversample` (default 4×) binary candidates, loads\n   their float32 vectors from the KV store, rescores by cosine, returns top-k. Ties break by\n   ascending key. This is standard production BQ practice (Qdrant/Weaviate) and is the step\n   the source research omitted; it lifts recall from ~0.9 to ≳0.99 at small k.","newString":"4. **Rescore (FR4):** search fetches `k × oversample` (default 8×) binary candidates, loads\n   their float32 vectors from the KV store, rescores by cosine, returns top-k. Ties break by\n   ascending key. This is standard production BQ practice (Qdrant/Weaviate) and is the step\n   the source research omitted. Measured on the G0 corpus (2,000 docs, 32 tight clusters,\n   768-dim, k=10, exact binary candidates): oversample 4 → recall 0.80; **oversample 8 →\n   recall 1.00** (stable through 24×). The 4× setting under-covers a ~62-doc cluster\n   neighborhood; 8× covers it. The parity phase (PLAN P3) must re-measure this dial on real\n   Prime-Silo embeddings before any toggle flip.","originalFile":"# ADR-002 — Vector index: 1-bit quantization, Hamming HNSW (USearch), float rescore\n\n**Status:** Accepted · 2026-07-03\n\n## Context\n\nFull-precision 768-dim float32 vectors cost 3,072 bytes each; at the 1M-vector design target\nthat is ~3 GB of index-resident data — hostile to edge boxes. Sign-based 1-bit quantization\ncompresses each vector to 96 packed bytes (32× reduction) and turns similarity into Hamming\ndistance, computable with hardware popcount. The normalized Hamming distance between two\nsign-quantized vectors is an unbiased estimator of the angular distance between the originals,\nso semantic ordering is approximately preserved (90–95% raw recall at this dimensionality).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Quantization** (`s16.quantize`): `packed = np.packbits(x > 0)`. Popcount via\n   `np.bitwise_count` (numpy ≥ 2.0).\n2. **Two index backends behind one interface** (`s16.index`):\n   - `UsearchBinaryIndex` — USearch HNSW, `ndim=768, metric=Hamming, dtype=B1`. The scale\n     backend: memory-mapped `view()` restore (zero-RAM load), single-header C++ under the hood.\n   - `BruteForceBinaryIndex` — exact numpy XOR+popcount scan. The deterministic reference:\n     bit-identical index files, exact results, no tuning. At the current real workload\n     (75k × 96 bytes ≈ 7 MB) this is already sub-millisecond, so it is a *credible* fallback,\n     not a toy.\n3. **Determinism (NFR1):** all USearch `add` calls run with `threads=1`. HNSW topology depends\n   on insertion order; parallel builds are non-reproducible by construction. We trade build\n   speed for bit-for-bit repeatability, per the source research's own recommendation.\n4. **Rescore (FR4):** search fetches `k × oversample` (default 4×) binary candidates, loads\n   their float32 vectors from the KV store, rescores by cosine, returns top-k. Ties break by\n   ascending key. This is standard production BQ practice (Qdrant/Weaviate) and is the step\n   the source research omitted; it lifts recall from ~0.9 to ≳0.99 at small k.\n\n## Alternatives rejected\n\n- **FAISS** — ~84k SLoC, BLAS/OpenMP dependencies, stiff 63-bit keys; wrong weight class for\n  an embedded zero-service store. USearch is ~3k SLoC, header-only, unrestricted metrics.\n- **Product quantization / learned codebooks** — better recall per bit but training state\n  breaks determinism and portability; sign quantization is stateless.\n- **\"MonaVec\"** (cited by the source research) — no verifiable engine of this name exists;\n  treated as a synthetic citation and ignored. Its stated principle (deterministic\n  single-threaded builds) is adopted on its own merits.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Recall gate: the feasibility use case enforces recall@10 ≥ 0.90 vs exact float cosine on\n  both backends (with rescore it should sit well above).\n- USearch index files are versioned by the library; `manifest.json` records backend + ndim so\n  a store reopens with the same configuration or fails loudly.\n- The oversample factor is the recall/latency dial; benchmarks record both settings so the\n  Prime-Silo parity phase (PLAN P3) tunes on data, not vibes.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":25,"oldLines":10,"newStart":25,"newLines":14,"lines":[" 3. **Determinism (NFR1):** all USearch `add` calls run with `threads=1`. HNSW topology depends","    on insertion order; parallel builds are non-reproducible by construction. We trade build","    speed for bit-for-bit repeatability, per the source research's own recommendation.","-4. **Rescore (FR4):** search fetches `k × oversample` (default 4×) binary candidates, loads","+4. **Rescore (FR4):** search fetches `k × oversample` (default 8×) binary candidates, loads","    their float32 vectors from the KV store, rescores by cosine, returns top-k. Ties break by","    ascending key. This is standard production BQ practice (Qdrant/Weaviate) and is the step","-   the source research omitted; it lifts recall from ~0.9 to ≳0.99 at small k.","+   the source research omitted. Measured on the G0 corpus (2,000 docs, 32 tight clusters,","+   768-dim, k=10, exact binary candidates): oversample 4 → recall 0.80; **oversample 8 →","+   recall 1.00** (stable through 24×). The 4× setting under-covers a ~62-doc cluster","+   neighborhood; 8× covers it. The parity phase (PLAN P3) must re-measure this dial on real","+   Prime-Silo embeddings before any toggle flip."," "," ## Alternatives rejected"," "]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8616a21d-81cf-4d82-a68d-e6d2373b1387","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"C:\\Users\\nsdha\\OneDrive\\binary16\\s16","sessionId":"7aba7344-6e98-4c00-b7e9-ad132cfe087b","version":"2.1.197","gitBranch":"HEAD"}
