2/cluster) | — | 0.61 | 0.81 | **0.998** | 1.00 |\n\n   The recall knee tracks a candidate *count* tied to corpus density, not a multiple of k —\n   a fixed oversample silently degrades as the corpus grows (0.58 observed in the first G1\n   bench run at 10k docs with 8× only). The `min_candidates` floor (384 > both knees; 0.5%\n   of the real 75k-concept workload) absorbs density variation; synthetic tight clusters are\n   near-worst-case for 1-bit discrimination. The parity phase (PLAN P3) must re-measure both\n   dials on real Prime-Silo embeddings before any toggle flip.","originalFile":"# ADR-002 — Vector index: 1-bit quantization, Hamming HNSW (USearch), float rescore\n\n**Status:** Accepted · 2026-07-03\n\n## Context\n\nFull-precision 768-dim float32 vectors cost 3,072 bytes each; at the 1M-vector design target\nthat is ~3 GB of index-resident data — hostile to edge boxes. Sign-based 1-bit quantization\ncompresses each vector to 96 packed bytes (32× reduction) and turns similarity into Hamming\ndistance, computable with hardware popcount. The normalized Hamming distance between two\nsign-quantized vectors is an unbiased estimator of the angular distance between the originals,\nso semantic ordering is approximately preserved (90–95% raw recall at this dimensionality).\n\n## Decision\n\n1. **Quantization** (`s16.quantize`): `packed = np.packbits(x > 0)`. Popcount via\n   `np.bitwise_count` (numpy ≥ 2.0).\n2. **Two index backends behind one interface** (`s16.index`):\n   - `UsearchBinaryIndex` — USearch HNSW, `ndim=768, metric=Hamming, dtype=B1`. The scale\n     backend: memory-mapped `view()` restore (zero-RAM load), single-header C++ under the hood.\n   - `BruteForceBinaryIndex` — exact numpy XOR+popcount scan. The deterministic reference:\n     bit-identical index files, exact results, no tuning. At the current real workload\n     (75k × 96 bytes ≈ 7 MB) this is already sub-millisecond, so it is a *credible* fallback,\n     not a toy.\n3. **Determinism (NFR1):** all USearch `add` calls run with `threads=1`. HNSW topology depends\n   on insertion order; parallel builds are non-reproducible by construction. We trade build\n   speed for bit-for-bit repeatability, per the source research's own recommendation.\n4. **Rescore (FR4):** search fetches `k × oversample` (default 8×) binary candidates, loads\n   their float32 vectors from the KV store, rescores by cosine, returns top-k. Ties break by\n   ascending key. This is standard production BQ practice (Qdrant/Weaviate) and is the step\n   the source research omitted. Measured on the G0 corpus (2,000 docs, 32 tight clusters,\n   768-dim, k=10, exact binary candidates): oversample 4 → recall 0.80; **oversample 8 →\n   recall 1.00** (stable through 24×). The 4× setting under-covers a ~62-doc cluster\n   neighborhood; 8× covers it. The parity phase (PLAN P3) must re-measure this dial on real\n   Prime-Silo embeddings before any toggle flip.\n\n## Alternatives rejected\n\n- **FAISS** — ~84k SLoC, BLAS/OpenMP dependencies, stiff 63-bit keys; wrong weight class for\n  an embedded zero-service store. USearch is ~3k SLoC, header-only, unrestricted metrics.\n- **Product quantization / learned codebooks** — better recall per bit but training state\n  breaks determinism and portability; sign quantization is stateless.\n- **\"MonaVec\"** (cited by the source research) — no verifiable engine of this name exists;\n  treated as a synthetic citation and ignored. Its stated principle (deterministic\n  single-threaded builds) is adopted on its own merits.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- Recall gate: the feasibility use case enforces recall@10 ≥ 0.90 vs exact float cosine on\n  both backends (with rescore it should sit well above).\n- USearch index files are versioned by the library; `manifest.json` records backend + ndim so\n  a store reopens with the same configuration or fails loudly.\n- The oversample factor is the recall/latency dial; benchmarks record both settings so the\n  Prime-Silo parity phase (PLAN P3) tunes on data, not vibes.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":25,"oldLines":15,"newStart":25,"newLines":25,"lines":[" 3. **Determinism (NFR1):** all USearch `add` calls run with `threads=1`. HNSW topology depends","    on insertion order; parallel builds are non-reproducible by construction. We trade build","    speed for bit-for-bit repeatability, per the source research's own recommendation.","-4. **Rescore (FR4):** search fetches `k × oversample` (default 8×) binary candidates, loads","-   their float32 vectors from the KV store, rescores by cosine, returns top-k. Ties break by","-   ascending key. This is standard production BQ practice (Qdrant/Weaviate) and is the step","-   the source research omitted. Measured on the G0 corpus (2,000 docs, 32 tight clusters,","-   768-dim, k=10, exact binary candidates): oversample 4 → recall 0.80; **oversample 8 →","-   recall 1.00** (stable through 24×). The 4× setting under-covers a ~62-doc cluster","-   neighborhood; 8× covers it. The parity phase (PLAN P3) must re-measure this dial on real","-   Prime-Silo embeddings before any toggle flip.","+4. **Rescore (FR4):** search fetches binary candidates, loads their float32 vectors from the","+   KV store, rescores by cosine, returns top-k (ties break by ascending key). Standard","+   production BQ practice (Qdrant/Weaviate) — the step the source research omitted."," ","+   **Candidate breadth = `max(k × oversample, min_candidates)`** (defaults 8× / 384), capped","+   at corpus size. Measured with exact binary candidates (k=10, 768-dim, 32 tight clusters):","+","+   | Corpus | candidates=40 | 80 | 160 | 320 | 640 |","+   | ------ | ------------- | -- | --- | --- | --- |","+   | 2,000 docs (~62/cluster) | 0.80 | **1.00** | 1.00 | 1.00 | 1.00 |","+   | 10,000 docs (~312/cluster) | — | 0.61 | 0.81 | **0.998** | 1.00 |","+","+   The recall knee tracks a candidate *count* tied to corpus density, not a multiple of k —","+   a fixed oversample silently degrades as the corpu